{
  "notes": "Phase 167.1.2 C2 round 2, R2-WR-01. ONE table both runtimes assert against, so the Python writers and the TS reader cannot drift apart silently. Book: key O (its own account, working, stamped), holder H (stamped with the shared account id) and key M marked `kind` against H with a NULL venue_account_id (the production shape). All three hold returns on the same three days, so no stitch applies. `states` are the key-row overrides each side applies. derive: `refuse` = the curve is deleted (account_duplicate); `compose` = the keys counted (by anchor) and whether the payload is trustworthy. reader: equityHistoryReadiness given the series the derive leaves (present when compose+trustworthy, else null with the reason the producer passes). The invariant this pins: the reader says duplicate_account exactly when the derive refuses, and it says ready only where the derive counts the shared account at most once on a trustworthy curve. Hand-written from D-18 (working = active, connected, not revoked/sign_in_failed/error; eligible = active, connected, not revoked), D-01/D-04 (a duplicate behind a WORKING holder is refused; a composite member never is) and the round-2 rule that a pair with no working key is untrustworthy (SFH-R2-04).",
